findings.suggestion:
Remove the property from application.properties (it does nothing). In SecurityConfig (Task 2), configure session cookie flags programmatically:

  @Bean
  public WebSessionIdResolver webSessionIdResolver() {
      CookieWebSessionIdResolver resolver = new CookieWebSessionIdResolver();
      resolver.addCookieInitializer(builder -> builder
          .httpOnly(true)
          .sameSite("Strict")  // or Lax for dev
          .path("/")
      );
      return resolver;
  }

This is the correct WebFlux approach documented in Spring Session Data MongoDB reactive docs.

Add a coverage enforcement block and wire it into the check lifecycle:

  jacocoTestCoverageVerification {
      dependsOn test
      violationRules {
          rule {
              element = 'PACKAGE'
              includes = ['com.botfunnel.auth.*', 'com.botfunnel.user.*', 'com.botfunnel.profile.*', 'com.botfunnel.email.*', 'com.botfunnel.events.*']
              limit {
                  counter = 'LINE'
                  value = 'COVEREDRATIO'
                  minimum = 0.80
              }
          }
      }
  }

  check.dependsOn jacocoTestCoverageVerification

This can be deferred to the test audit task (Task 14) if the intent is to add enforcement only after integration tests are written.
